ys a bit surprising when  you get something really good from  someone you've never heard of but then  to find out that he's 17 in high school  was mind-blowing  one thing I think is interesting about  my proof is that it really relies on  Fourier analysis which is a theory from  physics about decomposing functions into  waves foreign  set of objects and you want to study  subsets with some particular nice  property where you analysis lets you  study the set of subsets of the objects  and so that gives you this sort of tree  of possibilities expanding from this set  and now with very basic statements about  the trunk of that tree Fourier analysis  lets you make very expansive claims  about the branches my problem the trunk  was a certain set of prime numbers and I  wanted to show that you could sort of  predict the location of the apples or  the Carmichael numbers  there are many questions we still don't  know the answer to about Carmichael  numbers and actually some of them are  very relevant for cryptography modern  cryptography uses really large primes to  decrypt messages and so to find really  large primes the best thing to do is  just test random numbers for primality  and the problem is these Carmichael  numbers can register as primes even  though they're not and that can lead to  weaker messages so we would like to  understand Carmichael numbers better so  that we can avoid weaker encryption  one thing I really appreciated is I have  been hearing the names Maynard Granville  for palmerance for so many years these  are like Legends but you know a random  person emailed them and they responded  and they looked at the work and I'm just  so incredibly grateful he was doing work  at the level of a top quality PhD  student this super surprised me I mean  this is definitely  out of the ordinary even for you know  the US has a couple of prodigies every  year in mathematics right but but he's  exceptional even amongst them  I think this is really a great thing  about 